:: \ Aerobic Attempt {"position":"650,1100","size":"100,100"}
⚠️ PARTIALLY CORRECT — but not ideal right now!

Under aerobic conditions, pyruvate WOULD enter 
the mitochondria via Pyruvate Dehydrogenase 
→ forming Acetyl-CoA → TCA Cycle.

BUT — oxygen is LIMITED during this sprint! 

The TCA cycle and oxidative phosphorylation 
are running SLOWLY due to low O₂.

If the cell relies only on this route, 
glycolysis stalls because NADH 
cannot be reoxidised fast enough.

The sprinter slows dramatically... 😰

⚡ ATP Bank: -1 ATP (glycolysis stalls)

[[🔁 Reconsider -> Pyruvate Decision]]


:: \ Anaerobic Correct {"position":"525,1100","size":"100,100"}
✅ CORRECT! — Smart metabolic thinking! 🧠

Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) converts pyruvate 
to LACTATE, simultaneously oxidising NADH → NAD⁺

This regenerates NAD⁺ and keeps 
glycolysis running at FULL SPEED! 🔄

⚠️ No extra ATP is made here — 
but glycolysis can continue!

This is why lactate builds up during a sprint 
— it's not a waste product, it's a SURVIVAL STRATEGY! 💡

The lactate will be recycled later 
(Cori Cycle — liver converts it back to glucose!)

⚡ ATP Bank: +2 ATP (glycolysis keeps running!)
🏃 The sprinter pushes through the burn!

[[➡️ The Final Push — Oxygen Returns -> Oxygen Returns]]


:: \ Fermentation Wrong {"position":"775,1100","size":"100,100"}
❌ WRONG!

Ethanol fermentation happens in YEAST 🍺 — 
not in human muscle cells!

Imagine if your muscles produced alcohol 
during exercise... 😂

Human muscles use LACTATE, not ethanol, 
to regenerate NAD⁺.

⚡ ATP Bank: -2 ATP 😬
🏃 The sprinter trips over their own feet...

[[🔁 Try again -> Pyruvate Decision]]


:: \ Final Score {"position":"400,1475","size":"100,100"}
🏆 METABOLIC QUEST COMPLETE!
==============================

The sprint is over. Let's count your ATP...

🧮 SCORING SUMMARY:
  • Glycogen Breakdown:      +1 ATP
  • Glycolysis:              +2 ATP
  • Anaerobic phase:         +2 ATP  
  • Aerobic recovery:       +30 ATP
                           --------
  • MAXIMUM SCORE:          ~35 ATP 🏆

💡 KEY LESSONS FROM THIS SPRINT:

1️⃣ Muscle cells use BOTH aerobic and 
   anaerobic pathways — often simultaneously

2️⃣ Lactate is NOT a waste product — 
   it's a clever NAD⁺ recycling strategy

3️⃣ The oxygen debt explains why you 
   breathe hard AFTER exercise stops

4️⃣ Glycolysis is fast but low yield — 
   the TCA + OxPhos is slow but high yield

5️⃣ The balance between these pathways 
   depends on OXYGEN AVAILABILITY

🎓 Well done — you've just survived 
   a 100m sprint at the molecular level!

[[🔁 Play Again ->The start line]]


:: \ Glycolysis {"position":"775,725","size":"100,100"}
🔥 STAGE 1: GLYCOLYSIS
========================

Glucose-6-phosphate enters glycolysis 
in the cytoplasm.

The cell needs ATP NOW — oxygen supply 
is already becoming limited as the sprint intensifies.

Glycolysis splits glucose into two molecules of pyruvate.

What is the NET ATP yield from glycolysis 
of ONE glucose molecule?

[[🅰️ 2 ATP -> Glycolysis Correct]]
[[🅱️ 4 ATP -> Glycolysis Wrong 1]]
[[🅾️ 36 ATP -> Glycolysis Wrong 2]]


:: \ Glycolysis Correct {"position":"650,850","size":"100,100"}
✅ CORRECT!

Glycolysis invests 2 ATP and generates 4 ATP
— a NET gain of 2 ATP. ⚡

It also produces:
  • 2x Pyruvate 🧪
  • 2x NADH (electron carriers, useful later!)
  • 2x ATP (net)

This happens in the CYTOPLASM — 
no oxygen needed! Perfect for a sprint. 💨

⚡ ATP Bank: +2 ATP
🏃 The sprinter hits full stride!

[[➡️ What happens to Pyruvate? -> Pyruvate Decision]]


:: \ Glycolysis Hint {"position":"775,975","size":"100,100"}
💡 HINT: The Two Phases of Glycolysis
=======================================

Glycolysis has two distinct phases:

⬇️ INVESTMENT PHASE (costs ATP):
  • Glucose is phosphorylated TWICE
  • Costs 2 ATP

⬆️ PAYOFF PHASE (makes ATP):
  • Each 3-carbon molecule generates 2 ATP
  • Two 3-carbon molecules = 4 ATP total

🧮 NET YIELD = ?

<img src="https://i.vgy.me/TZbatH.jpg" 
style="width:100%; max-width:500px;">

[[🔁 Back to Question -> Glycolysis]]


:: \ Glycolysis Wrong 1 {"position":"775,850","size":"100,100"}
❌ NOT QUITE!

4 ATP is the GROSS yield — but remember, 
glycolysis has TWO phases...

An INVESTMENT phase and a PAYOFF phase.

Think carefully about the net result. 😅

⚡ ATP Bank: 0
🏃 The sprinter loses a step...

[[🔁 Try Again -> Glycolysis]]
[[💡 View Hint -> Glycolysis Hint]]


:: \ Glycolysis Wrong 2 {"position":"900,850","size":"100,100"}
❌ WHOOPS!

36-38 ATP is the yield for the ENTIRE 
aerobic respiration pathway — not just glycolysis!

Think smaller — glycolysis is just 
the FIRST stage of metabolism.

⚡ ATP Bank: -1 ATP 😬
🏃 The sprinter is struggling...

[[🔁 Try Again -> Glycolysis]]
[[💡 View Hint -> Glycolysis Hint]]


:: \ Lactate Correct {"position":"400,1350","size":"100,100"}
✅ BRILLIANT! 🌟

Lactate Dehydrogenase works in REVERSE 
now that oxygen is available:

Lactate → Pyruvate + NADH

Pyruvate then enters the mitochondria:
  • Pyruvate Dehydrogenase → Acetyl-CoA
  • Acetyl-CoA → TCA Cycle 🔄
  • TCA → NADH + FADH₂
  • Oxidative Phosphorylation → ~30 ATP ⚡⚡⚡

This is why you keep breathing hard 
AFTER exercise stops — you're repaying 
the oxygen debt and processing all that lactate!

⚡ ATP Bank: +30 ATP 🎉
🏃 Full recovery underway!

[[➡️ See your final score -> Final Score]]


:: \ Lactate Wrong 1 {"position":"525,1350","size":"100,100"}
❌ NOT QUITE!

A small amount of lactate IS lost 
in sweat and urine, but this is negligible.

The MAJORITY of lactate is a valuable 
metabolic fuel — the body doesn't waste it!

Most lactate is:
  • Recycled in the muscle itself, OR
  • Sent to the liver (Cori Cycle) 
    to be converted back to glucose

⚡ ATP Bank: 0
[[🔁 Try again -> Oxygen Returns]]


:: \ Lactate Wrong 2 {"position":"650,1350","size":"100,100"}
❌ NOT QUITE!

Lactate cannot be converted DIRECTLY to ATP.

It must first be converted back to Pyruvate,
then processed through the TCA cycle 
and oxidative phosphorylation to yield ATP.

There are no shortcuts in metabolism! 😄

⚡ ATP Bank: -1 ATP
[[🔁 Try again -> Oxygen Returns]]


:: \ Oxygen Returns {"position":"525,1225","size":"100,100"}
🌬️ STAGE 3: CROSSING THE FINISH LINE
=======================================

The sprinter crosses the finish line! 🏁

They're bent over, breathing HARD. 
Oxygen is now flooding back into the muscle.

The lactate that built up during the sprint 
is now being dealt with.

This is called the "oxygen debt" 
or EPOC (Excess Post-exercise Oxygen Consumption).

What happens to the LACTATE now 
that oxygen is available again?

[[🅰️ Lactate is converted back to Pyruvate and enters the TCA Cycle -> Lactate Correct]]
[[🅱️ Lactate is excreted in urine -> Lactate Wrong 1]]
[[🅾️ Lactate is converted directly to ATP -> Lactate Wrong 2]]


:: \ Pyruvate Decision {"position":"650,975","size":"100,100"}
⚖️ STAGE 2: THE OXYGEN CRISIS
================================

Two pyruvate molecules are ready. 
But here's the problem...

The sprint is 70 metres in. 
Oxygen delivery CANNOT keep up 
with the muscle's demand. 😤

The cell faces a critical decision:

The NADH produced in glycolysis needs 
to be regenerated back to NAD⁺ — 
otherwise glycolysis STOPS.

How does the muscle cell regenerate NAD⁺ 
when oxygen is LIMITED?

[[🅰️ Convert pyruvate to Lactate (Lactate Dehydrogenase) -> Anaerobic Correct]]
[[🅱️ Send pyruvate to the mitochondria for the TCA cycle -> Aerobic Attempt]]
[[🅾️ Convert pyruvate to Ethanol -> Fermentation Wrong]]


:: Dip into the easy-access store {"position":"900,475","size":"100,100"}
⚡ STAGE 0: MOBILISING FUEL
=============================

The muscle cell has no free glucose yet.
Energy stores are locked up as GLYCOGEN.

A signal arrives — adrenaline is flooding the bloodstream! 🚨

The cell needs to break glycogen down to enter metabolism.

Which enzyme is responsible for breaking glycogen 
down into glucose-1-phosphate?

[[🅰️ Glycogen Phosphorylase ->Glycogen Correct]]
[[🅱️ Glycogen Synthase ->Glycogen Wrong 1]]
[[🅾️ Hexokinase ->Glycogen Wrong 2]]


:: Glycogen Correct {"position":"775,600","size":"100,100"}
✅ CORRECT!

Glycogen Phosphorylase cleaves glucose units 
from glycogen, producing glucose-1-phosphate.

This is quickly converted to glucose-6-phosphate 
— skipping the need for hexokinase and 
SAVING 1 ATP! 💰

⚡ ATP Bank: +1 ATP (saved, not spent!)
🏃 The sprinter accelerates!

[[➡️ Enter Glycolysis -> Glycolysis]]


:: Glycogen Wrong 1 {"position":"900,600","size":"100,100"}
❌ NOT QUITE!

Glycogen Synthase does the OPPOSITE — 
it BUILDS glycogen, it doesn't break it down.

The cell wastes precious milliseconds. 
The sprinter stumbles slightly. 😬

⚡ ATP Bank: 0 (no gain)

[[🔁 Try again ->Dip into the easy-access store]]


:: Glycogen Wrong 2 {"position":"1025,600","size":"100,100"}
❌ CLOSE, BUT NO!

Hexokinase phosphorylates FREE glucose — 
but glycogen must be broken down FIRST 
before hexokinase can act.

Also, using hexokinase here would 
COST 1 ATP rather than save it!

⚡ ATP Bank: -1 ATP 😬
🏃 The sprinter feels the burn early...

[[🔁 Try again ->Dip into the easy-access store]]


:: The start line {"position":"900,350","size":"100,100"}
🏃 METABOLIC QUEST: THE SPRINT
================================

It's race day.

You are a metabolic regulator, quietly idling in a muscle cell of an elite sprinter.

The starting pistol fires. 🔫

Suddenly, the cell is in CRISIS. ATP levels are 
dropping FAST. The muscle is screaming for energy.

Your mission: guide glucose through metabolism, 
make smart decisions, and keep this sprinter moving.

⚡ Every correct choice = ATP gained
☠️ Every wrong choice = ATP lost + the sprinter slows down

Your ATP Bank: 0 ATP

Are you ready?

[[🏁 Begin the Sprint ->Dip into the easy-access store]]
